blocks:
  - type: metin
    metin: |-
      The short answer: yes. "Is online therapy effective?" has become one of the most rigorously tested questions in psychology research. A [Cochrane systematic review](https://www.cochrane.org/CD002098/EPOC_interactive-telemedicine-effects-professional-practice-and-health-care-outcomes) (Flodgren et al., 2015) found no difference in outcome between therapy delivered over videoconferencing and therapy delivered in the room.

      In this article we look at how the bond with your therapist travels through a screen, who online therapy suits, and where the limits of remote support begin.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: What does the research show?
    giris: 'The effectiveness of online therapy is one of the most tested questions in the field. The common finding across independent reviews:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: No difference found between video and in-person therapy
        metin: "A [Cochrane systematic review](https://www.cochrane.org/CD002098/EPOC_interactive-telemedicine-effects-professional-practice-and-health-care-outcomes) (Flodgren et al., 2015) examined seven studies covering a range of mental health and substance use problems. It found no difference in outcome between therapy delivered over videoconferencing and therapy delivered face to face."
      - baslik: Not every condition has been studied to the same depth
        metin: The strength of the evidence is not the same for every presentation, and in some areas the number of studies is still small. That is why online therapy is treated not as a one-size-fits-all solution but as an option we weigh together against your situation.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: What about the bond with your therapist? It travels through the screen too
    metin: One of the strongest predictors of whether therapy works is the relationship of trust between client and therapist — what the literature calls the "therapeutic alliance". Research shows this bond forms in video sessions as well; even in studies where alliance ratings measured slightly lower than in-person, treatment outcomes remained equivalent. Most clients say that after a few sessions they stop thinking about the format altogether.
  - type: metin
    baslik: Why does online therapy work so well?
    metin: |-
      Equivalent outcomes may seem surprising at first — until you look at what actually makes therapy effective. It isn't the room: the same trained therapist, the same evidence-based methods and the same regular rhythm of sessions carry over to the screen intact.

      Remote psychological support also has genuine advantages of its own. Joining from your own space makes opening up easier for some clients. With travel, traffic and waiting rooms gone, cancellations drop — and consistency is one of the strongest predictors of results in therapy. Living outside Famagusta, in a rural area or abroad no longer limits your access to qualified support.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: Getting the best results from online sessions
    giris: 'Another consistent research finding: it''s the conditions, not the format, that make the difference. These four simple preparations noticeably improve what you get out of an online session:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: Choose a private space
        aciklama: Your room, your car, a quiet study room — anywhere you know you won't be overheard. Feeling safe is the precondition for opening up.
      - baslik: Use headphones
        aciklama: They solve sound quality and privacy at the same time, and make it easier to keep your attention in the session.
      - baslik: Keep a fixed session time
        aciklama: Therapy draws its power from regularity. Sessions anchored to the same day and hour each week produce the best results in either format.
      - baslik: Leave 10 minutes after the session
        aciklama: The processing that happens naturally as you walk home from an in-person session — create it deliberately by not returning to work the moment you close the screen.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: 'Let''s be honest: there are situations where in-person therapy fits better'
    metin: Acute crisis periods and situations where safety risks require close monitoring, certain applications of specific therapy techniques, and clients who cannot find a private space at home may all be better served by starting face-to-face. Some people also value the ritual of leaving the house for a session — an entirely valid preference. At Mağusa Psikoloji Merkezi we offer both formats, and a blend of the two when useful. The practical details are on the [online therapy](/en/services/online-therapy.html) page.
  - type: metin
    baslik: 'The real question isn''t "does it work" — it''s "does it fit you"'
    metin: |-
      The scientific debate is largely settled; online therapy is as effective as face-to-face therapy for most common psychological difficulties. The remaining question is personal: which format fits your routine, your privacy situation and your preferences best?

      If you're curious how online sessions work in practice — from booking to between-session follow-up — see our [guide to online therapy in North Cyprus](/en/blog/online-therapy-north-cyprus.html). Still unsure whether therapy is the right step at all? [When to go to therapy](/en/blog/when-to-go-to-therapy.html) can help. In your first session we discuss the format together and choose what suits you.
